# CHAPTER 1 – GENERAL

**AIM**

- The purpose of this QSP is to describe in operational terms the required outcome of individual training and education (IT&E) for Cyber Operators (CYBER OP) employed in the entry level job of Cyber Defence (Def) Analyst. This QSP is designed to assist the TE in developing, conducting, and evaluating the required IT&E.

**RESONSIBLE AGENCY AND TRAINING ESTABLISHMENTS**

- TA: MPGTG.

- TE: CTU.

**OUTLINE OF TRAINING**

- Commanders require personnel who possess the knowledge and skills necessary to perform the many tasks required of a CYBER OP employed as a Cyber Def Analyst. This job is performed in an operations centre in support of the Sea, Land and Air elements. The primary responsibility is to identify indicators of compromise through the analysis of Department of National Defence (DND)/Canadian Armed Forces (CAF)information technology (networks). The main tasks include collecting, examining, and analyzing technical and threat indicators, including the creation of technical reports.

- This QSP contains ten Performance Objectives (POs), PO 001 - 005 are held at an outsourced college and PO 006 - 010 are CAF provided training at the CTU.

**VALIDATION**

- This training documentation will be used for validation by MPGTG J7-7 Standards.

# CHAPTER 2 – PERFORMANCE OBJECTIVES

## PO 001 Maintain Network Situational Awareness

- Performance:** **Maintain network situational awareness.

- Conditions:

	- given:	

- target network connectivity;

- open-source internet connection;

- administrator credentials;

- IM/IT and ancillary equipment;

- network sensors;

- network assessment software and tools;

- network diagrams;

- references;

- suitable work area;

- supervision; and

- assistance;

				- denied: nil; and

				- environment: day or night, under any weather conditions, within a Network Operations Centre (NOC) environment.

- Standard: The Cyber Def Analyst shall maintain network situational awareness by:

	- enumerating the network and system by:

- preparing the network scanning tool, to include:

- identifying targets and Internet Protocol (IP) space;

- verifying targets and IP space as DND/CAF-owned; and

- configuring the network scanning tool, in accordance with (IAW) the tool documentation;

- (FT0290) testing custom network assessment functionality, to include:

- running the tool with current configurations on a test IP range;

- validating the test output to ensure correct results; and

- adjusting the scan configurations as required, to account for unforeseen issues with bandwidth usage, scan run time or other parameters;

- running a network scanning tool on target IP space, to include:

- initiating and monitoring scan progress;

- troubleshooting scan progress and reconfiguring scan, to include pausing, assessing results, modifying and resuming scan;

- validating scan output to ensure correct results; and

- processing the output, in order to achieve a readable or visual format;

- analyzing scan results, to include:

- identifying critical information, to include:

- (BT0410) identifying device applications and operating systems (OS) based on network traffic; and

- identifying other information, including device type, port status, IP, host name, etc.;

- fingerprinting the device, to include:

- characterizing the device by merging identifying information; and

- validating the fingerprint, if required, through confirmatory scans, secondary tools, or other information sources; and

- (ET0100) determining the location of physical network assets, to include:

- identifying location using automated tools (e.g., IP Control) or other network monitoring tools; and

- identifying location using alternative methods, such as checking the geolocation of other IPs in range, cross-referencing with historical scan data, and cross-referencing with other identified devices or information; and

- updating the network situational awareness database, IAW database usage and documentation; 

- (ET0090) developing a logical network map by:

- collecting network asset information, to include data from:

- network traffic;

- automated scanning; and

- partner organizations;

- building a network map, to include using:

- manual methods to build the map, (e.g., Microsoft Visio, Microsoft PowerPoint) or other means; and

- automated methods to build the map, including automated tools (e.g., ZenMap, LanState Pro) and scripts; and

- updating the network situational awareness database, IAW database usage and documentation;

- identifying network and system vulnerabilities by:

- preparing vulnerability assessment tools and assessment methodology, to include:

- assessing intake package and client documentation;

- identifying targets, IP space and scope of scan;

- verifying targets and IP space as DND/CAF-owned; 

- acquiring administrative credentials or equivalent to achieve scan results; and

- configuring the vulnerability assessment tool, IAW the tool documentation;

- (FT0290) testing custom network assessment functionality, to include:

- running the tool with current configurations on a test IP range;

- validating the test output to ensure correct results; and

- adjusting the scan configurations as required, to account for unforeseen issues with bandwidth usage, scan run time or other parameters;

- conducting tool-based vulnerability assessment, to include:

- initiating and monitoring scan progress;

- troubleshooting scan progress and reconfiguring scan, to include pausing, assessing results, modifying and resuming scan;

- validating scan output to ensure correct results; and

- processing the output, in order to achieve a readable or visual format;

- conducting manual vulnerability assessment, to include:

- liaising with subject matter experts (SMEs), system administrators, system operators, technical staff, Chief Information Officers (CIO)/Information System Security Officers (ISSO) and others;

- assessing network configuration and processes against industry best practices and standards IAW ref C18; and

- supporting the task lead in assessing security controls, including physical, procedural, personal and IT security of network or system IAW refs A3 (Chapters 4 - 7) and A4 (applicable system Security Orders) and site Security Orders;

- analyzing the manual and automated vulnerability assessment results, to include:

- reviewing the automated results’ criticality score (e.g., Common Vulnerabilities and Exposures [CVE] scores) IAW ref C19;

- conducting a combined assessment of the manual and automated results; and

- (BT0280) assessing extent of identified vulnerabilities; 

- (CT0050) initiating technical report; and

- updating the network situational awareness database, IAW database usage and documentation; and

- (GT0070) characterizing network traffic to establish normal patterns IAW ref C20 by:

- preparing for traffic collection, to include:

- identifying the network devices and security appliances;

- identifying the whitelisted, blacklisted and authorized traffic, protocols, applications, etc., for routine and non-routine operations;

- determining the period of time required to benchmark network activity; and

- preparing tools and scripts for collection;

- collecting network traffic, to include:

- using automated tools to capture network data, to include:

- initiating and monitoring packet capture;

- troubleshooting packet capture activities; and

- validating output to ensure intended results; and

- using other means to collect required network data, including scripts, commands, etc.;

- analyzing packet capture and ancillary data, to include:

- identifying distinct sessions and source/destination hosts;

- creating statistics; and

- summarizing baseline activity/metrics, to include:

- peak times and low times; and

- bandwidth usage and traffic volume by application, protocol, port, etc.; and

- (CT0050) initiating technical report; and

- presenting statistics.

- Remarks:

- this PO sees the Cyber Def Analyst presented with a network. They then conduct activities to establish and maintain situational awareness of the structure, composition, traffic patterns and security posture of the network. This is achieved through use of automated tools, manual methods and various levels of analysis of the raw data; and

- though this PO focuses on preparing the Cyber Def Analyst to maintain situational awareness of a network for the purposes of training and assessment, it is expected that they will be able to transfer acquired skills and knowledge to maintaining situational awareness of the cyber domain (defined as “all infrastructure, entities, users and activities related to, or affecting, cyberspace”).  This broader responsibility is articulated in Chapter 4 of this QSP. 

## PO 002 Respond to a Cyber Event

- Performance:** **Respond to a cyber event.

- Conditions:

				- given:	

- applicable network connections;

- applicable software;

- network diagrams;

- open-source internet connection;

- analyst workstation;

- references;

- supervision; and

- assistance;

				- denied: administrator rights to servers; and

				- environment: day or night, under any weather conditions, within a NOC environment.

- Standard: The Cyber Def Analyst shall respond to a cyber event (BT0350) by:

- detecting threat activity by:

- receiving Intrusion Detection System (IDS) alerts from various sources;

- (GT0250) analyzing IDS alerts from various sources IAW refs C35 and C116, to include:

- identifying traffic parameters IAW refs C57 and C59;

- extracting network traffic IAW refs C77, C78, C95, C96 and C125;

- (BT0361) extracting network indicators from IDS IAW refs C35 and C116;

- determining validity of alert IAW refs C76, C99, C105, C106 and C107; and

- logging false positives IAW ref C115;

- (GT0080) analyzing network traffic IAW refs C14, C58, C65, C97, C116 and C118, to include:

- (GT0110) analyzing anomalies in network traffic using metadata IAW refs C57 and C125; 

- (BT0370) identifying network mapping and operating system finger printing activities; 

- investigating traffic behavior IAW refs C78 and C125; 

- analyzing event logs from perimeter security appliances IAW refs C116 and C126; and

- (BT0361) extracting network indicators from traffic IAW refs C74, C78, C96 and C125;

- investigating cyber events by:

- validating alerts/anomalous behavior through network analysis, to include:

- (ET0060) collecting metadata, network traffic and logs, to include:

- (BT0410) identifying device applications and operating systems based on network traffic IAW refs C58, C78, C97, C116, C118 and C125; 

- identifying event timeline IAW refs C74, C116 and C125;

- (BT0361) extracting network indicators from traffic IAW refs C74, C78, C96 and C125; and

- (ET0100) determining location of physical network asset IAW refs C125, C127 and C128;

- (BT0420) reconstructing a malicious activity based on network traffic, to include:

- analyzing malicious activities IAW refs C74, C78 and C125;

- collating traffic activity to determine the source of compromise IAW refs C78, C116 and C125;

- (GT0040) identifying means of compromise, including command and control channels and exfiltration IAW refs C14, C70, C73, C108, C114, C125, D5, D7, D8 and D10;

- identifying exploitation attempts IAW refs C109, D7, D9 and D10; and

- extracting packet capture for protocol analysis IAW C78, C114, C125 and D20;

- providing preliminary findings for additional analysis, to include indicators (i.e., IPs, hostnames, ports); and

- (BT0560) identifying indicators of cyber activity;

- conducting packet capture (PCAP) analysis, to include:

- analyzing PCAPs IAW refs C57, C58, C74, C79 and D20;

- (BT0362) extracting artifacts IAW ref C74, C119 and C125; and

- (ET0050) collecting artifacts IAW ref C74;

- performing malware analysis IAW refs C74, C77 and C95, to include:

- analyzing artifacts IAW ref C120;

- characterizing malware with automated tools IAW refs C74, C77, C95 and C121; and

- (BT0210) transferring customer enquiries to appropriate sections;

- producing internal reports by:

- collating relevant data to (CT0050) initiate a technical report, to include:

- identifying or acknowledging the event or issue;

- creating a documented timeline of the incident; 

- identifying relevant findings and conclusions that must be communicated to the supervisor, external agency and/or client; and

- determining suitable recommendations to mitigate or resolve the event or issue;

- drafting the report, including the following elements as appropriate:

- any necessary introductory remarks/background information;

- observations/findings (e.g., risks and vulnerabilities, analysis findings, system and network/data facts);

- conclusions based on the observations/findings; and

- (CT0200) recommended mitigation measures to improve security and/or incident recovery measures;

- (ET0020) safeguarding cyber forensic evidence IAW ref D4 (Sections 3.11 – 3.15) by:

- creating a working copy of the artifact;

- preserving the original artifact;

- documenting specifically what the artifact is;

- documenting how it was obtained;

- documenting when it was collected;

- documenting who handled it and what actions have been taken; and

- documenting where the artifact is stored.

- Remarks:

- this PO sees the Cyber Def Analyst presented with an event to investigate using data collected from a variety of cyber defence tools (e.g., IDS alerts, firewalls, network traffic logs). The analyst will go through the steps to analyze the event that occurred within their environment for the purposes of mitigating threats through internal reporting; and

- task CT0130, document analysis notes, is not reflected specifically in one area of the PO as it applies in many areas. This task is conducted in parallel to other analysis activities and is difficult to discretely position it in one aspect of the standard.

- task CT0190, report analysis findings to client, is not reflected specifically in one area of the PO and is an aspect of producing technical reports.

## PO 003 Produce a Technical Report

- Performance:** **Produce a technical report. 

- Conditions:

				- given:	

						- report templates;

						- analyst workstation;

						- analyzed and raw data; 

						- other reports (internal and external);

						- supervision; and

						- assistance;

				- denied: nil; and

				- environment: day or night, under any weather conditions, within a NOC environment.

- Standard: The Cyber Def Analyst shall produce a basic technical report in order to inform on cyber threats (BT0220) IAW refs A1 (Pg. 2-003.01-1/2), A2 (Pg. 36, para 5.2 table 26), and D1 by:

- collating relevant data to initiate a technical report (CT0050) by:

- identifying or acknowledging the event or issue;

- creating a documented timeline of the event or issue; 

- extracting relevant details contained within document analysis notes (CT0130);

- (BT0580) producing a request for information (RFI);

- identifying relevant findings and conclusions that must be communicated to the supervisor, external agency and/or client; and

- determining suitable recommendations to mitigate or resolve the event or issue;

- drafting the report, including the following elements as appropriate:

- any necessary introductory remarks/background information;

- observations/findings (e.g., risks and vulnerabilities (CT0180), analysis findings (CT0190), system and network/data facts); 

- conclusions based on the observations/findings;

- (CT0200) recommending incident recovery measures to improve security; and

- (CT0060) updating technical report with any new information relevant to the event or issue that will increase the accuracy of the report;

- producing the report for verbal or written dissemination, taking into consideration the audience and use of appropriate technical language, employing the following principles:

- clarity (report is explicit, definite, complete, intelligible and unambiguous);

- accuracy (report is exact in detail and factual);

- relevance (report is free of irrelevant words, phrases and ideas);

- brevity (report is concise, ideas and facts are expressed as briefly as possible but not at the expense of clarity, accuracy, or relevance); and

- timeliness (report is delivered IAW required timelines).

- Remarks: This PO sees the Cyber Def Analyst produce a technical report using all raw available data, completed analyses and other relevant reports and cyber injects.

## PO 004 Prepare an Analysis Environment

- Performance:** **Prepare an analysis environment. 

- Conditions:

				- given:	

					- internet connection;

					- applicable operating systems;

					- applicable software;

						- applicable security hardware, routers, switch, firewall and sensors;

					- virtualization software;

					- virtualization hardware;

						- dual monitors (four monitors recommended);

						- configuration documents and assignments;

						- administrator credentials;

						- references;

						- supervision; and

						- assistance;

				- denied: nil; and

				- environment: day or night, under any weather conditions, within a NOC environment.

- Standard: The Cyber Def Analyst shall prepare an analysis environment by:

- (FT0010) installing hardware (e.g., workstation, hard drive, tap switch) IAW ref C21;

- (FT0030) configuring physical network security devices, to include:

- routers;

- switches;

- firewalls;

- network taps; and

- IDS and Intrusion Protection System (IPS);

- preparing and setting up virtual operating systems by:

- installing a virtual server (e.g., enterprise plus type 1 [ESXI]) IAW ref C43, to include:

- installing virtual Windows user workstation IAW refs C27 and C43;

- installing various workstation virtual images IAW refs C32 and C43;

- installing various mobile virtual images IAW ref C129; and

- setting up Windows servers, to include:

- domain controller IAW refs C28, C29 and C30;

- file and print server IAW refs C28, C29 and C30;

- Domain Name Server (DNS) IAW refs C28, C29, C30, C46 and C47;

- Dynamic Host Control Protocol (DHCP) server IAW refs C28, C29 and C30;

- exchange server IAW refs C28, C29, C30 and C48;

- Information Internet Server (IIS) Microsoft web server IAW refs C28, C29 and C30; and

- MySQL server IAW refs C28, C29 and C30;

- setting up security group policies, to include configuring IAW refs C27, C28, C29 and C30;

- setting up a Linux virtual workstation and server, to include:

- setting up Linux web server (e.g., Linux Apache MySQL and PHP (LAMP) web server) IAW ref C45;

- installing web application firewall (e.g., mod-security for Apache); and

- installing a Linux workstation IAW ref C31;

- setting up and configuring virtual networking systems, to include:

- routers IAW refs C23, C26 and C49;

- switches IAW refs C23, C26 and C49;

- network taps;

- IPS; and

- firewalls IAW refs C49 and C56; 

- managing snapshots of virtual images IAW ref C43, to include:

- taking virtual snapshots;

- reverting to old snapshots;

- reverting to new snapshots; and

- deleting snapshots;

- installing local virtual workstation environment (e.g., Virtual Box or VMware workstation), to include setting up a virtual operating system image (e.g., in .iso format) IAW ref C43;

- (FT0040) installing software, to include:

- user software (e.g., Adobe, Office, Wireshark);

- scripting software (e.g., Python, Ruby); and

- malware analysis tools (e.g., Apatedns, InetSim);

- (FT0060) configuring software (e.g., packet sniffing software);

- (FT0110) troubleshooting toolset hardware deficiencies by:

- replacing hardware; and 

- configuring physical networks;

- (FT0080) troubleshooting toolset software deficiencies by:

- resolving dependencies for software;

- uninstalling and installing applications; and

- installing and removing patches;

- setting up and maintaining network sensors by:

- setting up a network tap inline and out of band IAW ref C130, to include:

- installing a hardware tap; and

- mirroring a switch port (e.g., Hewlett Packard, CISCO);

- confirming network sensor functionality, to include:

- verifying full packet capture, to include full packet capture retention and when packets will roll over; 

- verifying metadata retention; and

- conducting sensor checks and reporting outages, to include informing supervisor of functionality;

- (FT0160) developing network sensor rules, to include:

- writing a network sensor rule;

- (FT0180) testing network sensor rule functionality; 

- (FT0200) recommending deployment of network sensor rules;

- (FT0130) loading network sensor rules to sensors; 

- (BT0380) validating intrusion detection system (IDS) alerts against network traffic; and

- (FT0230) recommending deactivation of network sensor rules;

-  (FT0050) removing software by:

- removing software from add-remove programs through Windows;

- removing software from Linux command line (e.g., with “apt-get and yum remove”); and

- formatting computer (e.g., with a kill disk); and

- (FT0020) removing hardware (e.g., switch, router, tap, USB, hard drive).

- Remarks:

- this PO prepares the Cyber Def Analyst to configure hardware and software. The analyst will be capable of installing, maintaining and removing cyber defence hardware and software and creating and removing IDS/IPS rules; and

- the setup and configuration of various security devices in a small network environment will prepare the analyst for custom applications and unique deployment requirements.

## PO 005 Develop Software Tools

- Performance:** **Develop software tools.

- Conditions:

				- given:	

						- analyst workstation;

						- Integrated Development Environment (IDE); 

						- unfiltered internet connection; 

						- supervision; and 

						- assistance.

				- denied: nil; and

				- environment: day or night, under any weather conditions, within a NOC environment.

- Standard: The Cyber Def Analyst shall develop software tools (FT0280) by:

- (FT0090) determining software deficiencies IAW ref C17 (Chapters 1, 2, 5, 6, and18);

- planning software tool development by: 

- translating security requirements into application design elements, to include:

- documenting the elements of software attack surfaces IAW refs D2 (Sections 1 and 2) and D3 (Section 3.2); and

- identifying security implications on the network IAW refs C16 (Chapter 8), D2 (Sections 2 and 4.2) and D3 (Section 3.2);

- preparing workflow charts, to include:

- implementing security into the software development cycle IAW ref C17 (Chapters 3–5); and

- producing programming flow chart IAW refs C12, C14 (Chapter 12), and C15 (Chapters 13, 14 and 22);

- building software tools by:

- writing software documentation IAW refs C15 (Chapters 3–5) and C17;

- programming custom algorithms, to include:

- designing algorithms, to include:

- identifying basic coding flaws, to include applying coding and testing standards IAW refs C14 (Chapter 12) and C16 (Chapter 1); and

- applying security and programming best practices, to include programming scripts and programs, IAW refs C5, C7, C8, C11, C12 and C15;

- applying code and testing standards, to include testing tools and conducting trial runs of programs, to include:

- debugging software and designing unit tests IAW refs C15 (Chapter 6) and C17; and

- correcting program errors, by making appropriate changes and rechecking to program to ensure desired results, IAW refs C15 and C17;

- maintaining software tools by:

- determining appropriate software patches, to include documenting software patches IAW ref C41;

- modifying existing software IAW refs C14 (Chapters 17 and 18), C41, C42 (Chapter 54) and D3 (Chapter 2), to include:

- correcting errors;

- upgrading interfaces; and

- improving performance; and

- performing risk analysis when an application has a major change IAW refs C14, C41, C42 (Chapter 54) and D3 (Chapter 2).

- Remarks: 

- this PO sees the Cyber Def Analyst able to design, build, test, and maintain custom software tools. They will be able to determine deficiencies in existing tools to design and build tools to resolve those deficiencies. They will have competency in scripting and be able to write small programs for the purpose of building custom tools. This PO is not intended to make Cyber Def Analysts expert at software development, but will give them basic scripting skills and provide foundational skills for other POs as well as future specialized job-based training; and

- FT0280, develop custom network analysis toolsets, was determined to be too restrictive by limiting the task to network analysis. Broadening the scope to “software tools” includes foundational skills/knowledge required for future specialized roles (Blue Analyst, Red Analyst, Digital Forensics Analyst, Cyber Support Analyst, and Threat Intelligence Analyst).

## PO 006 Analyze DND Network Traffic

- Performance: Analyze DND network traffic.

- Conditions:

				- given:

				- analyst workstation;

				- required software;

				- references;

				- applicable unclassified network connections; 

				- applicable classified network connections; 

				- applicable network diagrams; 

				- supervision; and 

				- assistance;

				- denied: administrator rights to classified servers; and

				- environment: day or night, under any weather conditions, within a NOC environment.

- Standard: The Cyber Def Analyst shall (GT0080) analyze DND network traffic by:

				- complying with DND security orders and directives to include:

			- describing DND units, associated organizations and policies;

			- describing DND security orders and directives;

			- describing security clearance process; and

		- managing classified materials; and

				- identifying DND network architecture.

- Remarks:

- the purpose of this PO is to enable the Cyber Def Analyst to apply the skills developed in the earlier POs in a CAF context. Using tools, networks and tradecraft specific to the CAF, they will monitor simulated DND/CAF network;

- task CT0130, document analysis notes, is not reflected specifically in one area of the PO as it applies in many areas. This task is conducted in parallel to other analysis activities and is difficult to discretely position it in one area of the PO. It is included in the list of trained tasks at paragraph 5;

- the CAF currently has limited NOC standard operating procedures (SOPs). While SOPs exist, they are largely undocumented. As SOPs are developed, additional references will need to be added to this PO. Additionally, personnel from the Canadian Forces Network Operations Centre (CFNOC) will be required to speak to the undocumented processes to inform training development;

- analyst workstation: defined as a non-baseline workstation (out-of-band) which includes network traffic analysis and command line tools. The Cyber Def Analyst must have local administrator privileges; and

- the DP1 cyber operator is expected to work under close supervision with extensive mentorship for multiple years before being able to independently find and investigate attacks. The DP1 analyst is not expected to be able to find advanced attacks or conduct in-depth investigations.

