- paper towel (roll/package).

## TRAINING LIMITATION

- Conduct of training must include an area with UNCLAS internet connectivity and a secure discussion area for PO 006. Training without access to these is not possible, due to the scale and complexity of the required training environments.

## COURSE CAPACITY

- To ensure maximum training effectiveness and efficiency and to meet safety standards, the following instructor/students’ ratios will be adhered to:

- for Mod 1, under the contracted program and as per contract W4938-20069S/001/ZH:

	- professors/instructors: (student/professor ratio) 12:1;

	- teaching assistants: (student/TA ratio) 12:1; and

	- facilities: (student/syndicate room ratio) 48:1.

- for Mod 2 - 4 at the Training Establishment:

	- instructors: (student/instructor ratio) 4:1;

	- student facilities: (student/syndicate room ratio) 12:1; and

	- equipment: classroom/computer lab requirements IAW paragraph 6.

	- A minimum of 2 instructors will be required to deliver the course.

- The capacity per course is:

- for Mod 1, under the contracted program and as per contract W4938-20069S/001/ZH:

	- optimal load – 24;

	- maximum load – 48; and

	- minimum load – 24.

	- for Mod 2 - 4 at the Training Establishment:

	- optimal load – 12;

	- maximum load – 12; and

	- minimum load – 4.

- Exceeding the maximum course capacity will require the following additional resources:

- for Mod 1, under the contracted program and as per contract W4938-20069S/001/ZH:

	- professors/instructors: (student/professor ratio) 12:1;

	- teaching assistants: (student/TA ratio) 12:1; and

	- facilities: (student/syndicate room ratio) 24:1.

- for Mod 2 - 4 at the Training Establishment:

	- instructors: (student/instructor ratio) 4:1;

	- student facilities: (student/syndicate room ratio) 12:1; and

	- equipment: increase in classroom/computer lab requirements by number of additional students IAW paragraph 6.

- The impact of not meeting the minimum course load is the serial will be rescheduled or cancelled.

- Permission is required from the TA to deviate from the prescribed course capacity.

## COURSE LOADING

- The authority to load members onto this course lies with the CYBER OP Career Manager, Requirements Advisor (RA) (D Cyber Ops FD/Comd CFIOG) and the TA.

## SCHEDULING

- Training will be scheduled by the CTU MITE, in consultation with MPGTG HQ, to meet the training requirements.

## PREREQUISITES

- To be selected for this course the loading authority must ensure that students have completed the Basic Military Qualification (BMQ) course (AINT).

- To be selected for Mods 2 - 4, students must pass the 124201 Foundational Knowledge/Skills Assessment (FKSA) in accordance with Annex E.

- Due to standard quality control issues on Module 1, the FKSA is required to ensure that students are ready to take Mods 2 - 4 of the Pte RQ.

- To combat skill-fade, this qualification is to expire after a year.

- To be selected for Mod 2 - 4, students must have an English language profile of BBB.

Remarks: Language profile is required in Mod 2 - 4 as official translated version of the course is currently unavailable along with necessary bilingual staff.

## QUALIFICATION

- Successful completion of the four training modules constitutes the primary means of attaining the CYBER OP Private RQ (ALJQ). Additionally, the following MITE course IDs will be granted:

- Module 1 – 121792;

- Module 2 – 121793;

- Module 3 – 121794; and

- Module 4 – 124023.

## ENVIRONMENTAL CONSIDERATIONS

- Nil.

## LANGUAGE OF INSTRUCTION

- The language of instruction shall be in accordance with the stated tasking contained in the "Official Languages Act" (DAOD 5039-6).

## EVALUATION

- Program evaluation shall be conducted IAW CFITES Volume 11 – Evaluation of Instructional Programmes.

## PRIOR LEARNING ASSESSMENT AND RECOGNITION (PLAR)

- PLARs shall be conducted in accordance with DAOD 5031-1 – Canadian Forces Military Equivalencies Program and CFITES Volume 12 – Canadian Forces Military Equivalencies Programme: Prior Learning Assessment.

## SPECIAL INSTRUCTIONS / REMARKS

- Nil.

## ASSESSMENT DETAILS

## GENERAL

- Performance checks (PC) and critical enabling checks (ECs) are based on the Performance Objective (PO) in Chapter 2, and the EOs in Chapter 4 of this QSP. The standards identified in the POs must be met to achieve this qualification.

## ENABLING CHECKS (ECs)

- ECs are practical or written tests that help the student, and the instructors recognize progress or gaps in learning, so that confirmation or corrective action (such as additional practice or remedial instruction) can be provided. They also reinforce learning, so that it will be better retained for longer periods. ECs are designed to give feedback to both the instructor(s) and students. Marks for non-critical ECs are **not** calculated in the student’s final mark.

## CRITICAL ENABLING CHECKS

- For the purposes of this QSP, critical ECs are written, or practical summative tests used to determine if the student has met the knowledge or skill-based requirements necessary to continue to the next step. Supplemental critical ECs are administered upon failure of a critical EC and must be administered in the same format as the original critical EC.

## PERFORMANCE CHECKS (PCs)

- PCs are summative tests used to determine if the student has met the standard under the conditions of the PO (where possible) and hence provide proof that the student can do the job. When supplemental PCs are administered upon failure of a PC they must be in the same format and have the same standard as the original PC.

## RESPONSIBILITY FOR TESTING

- Assessment of students shall be conducted IAW CFITES Volume 7 – Evaluation of Learners. 

- Standards provide quality control by administering and scoring PCs. Any PC not administered or invigilated by standards staff will be administered by a non-involved instructor designated by the senior instructor. ECs are the responsibility of the Instructional Staff. 

## PROGRESS MONITORING

- Monitoring learning is the responsibility of all instructional and standards staff and instructional supervisors. Monitoring is done IAW CFITES Volume 6 – Conduct of Instruction Programmes and TE SOPs.

- Monitoring is required in order to provide the following:

- student interview and discussion of progress to date.  A student who experiences difficulty is to be informed of the consequences of further failure(s) or marginal assessment and disposition options such as retraining, occupational transfer, return to unit (RTU) or release;

- feedback on the effectiveness of the training design; and

- information for Progress Review Boards (PRBs). PRBs are conducted IAW Training Group Standing Orders (TGSO) - 1116E. As a result of poor performance or conduct, a member may appear before a PRB. The PRB will make a recommendation to the Commandant regarding the disposition of the student. Recommended action will normally be one of the following:

- continue training with or without reassessment;

- continue training with or without remedial instruction;

- re-course;

- cease training and return to unit; and

- cease training and referred to Personnel Selection Officer (PSO)

- Student Record file: A student's record file shall be maintained and reflect the following:

- completion of essential training activities required by POs;

- results of PCs, ECs, and critical ECs/written tests and other assessments as specified in the assessment plan at Annex E;

- identification of elements requiring observations such as leadership, participation, qualities of character (e.g. NCM-like or professional qualities) and / or other related assessment factors; and

- interviewing / counselling results.

- Course Critiques: Sessions will be held to obtain feedback on learning activities, the presentation of training programme content and administration procedures.  This does not preclude the class senior from commenting on positive matters or reporting problems as they arise.

## REMOVAL FROM TRAINING

- The Commandant may direct that a student be removed from training:

- when the student's progress is below the minimum standard and there is virtually no likelihood that the required standard will be attained; or

- when the student's continued presence is adversely affecting the training, safety or morale of the other students; and/or

- for administrative or disciplinary reasons.

- When a student is removed from the course for any reason, the TE shall ensure that this information is recorded in MITE.

## REPORTS

- Formal Course: A Canadian Forces Course Report, Form CF 377, shall be prepared and distributed in accordance with DAOD 5031-9. The course report shall be descriptive, identifying the student's strengths and weaknesses, such as:

- regard for the safety of others;

- ability to work with others in a team environment;

- ability to work under pressure;

- attention to military protocol;

- due diligence with regard to cost savings in materials;

- oral and written expression; and

- care and attention to detail.

- On-Job Performance Requirement: Nil.

- Remarks: Nil.

# CHAPTER 4 – ENABLING OBJECTIVES

PO 006 Analyze DND Network Traffic

EO 006.01 Comply with DND Security Orders and Directives

- Performance: Comply with DND security orders and directives.

- Conditions:

				- given:

					- supervision;

					- applicable networks; and

					- applicable references;

				- denied:

- assistance;

- references;

- internet; and

- notes;

				- environment: suitable Level II classroom/computer lab environment.

- Standard: IAW specified references, the student shall comply with DND security orders and directives by:

				- describing DND units, associated organizations and policies;

				- describing DND security orders and directives;

				- describing security clearance process; and

				- managing classified materials.

- Time Required:

				- EO - 11 x 50 min periods; and

				- EC - 2 x 60 min period.

- Instructional Method: Interactive lecture.

- Training Aids:

- display connected to instructor workstation;

- whiteboard, with markers and eraser;

- PowerPoint slides containing key information related to lessons (e.g., definitions, diagrams, etc.);

- CSNI account; and

- CSNI workstation.

- Test Details: See Annex E, Assessment Plan.

- Remarks: Nil.

- Teaching Points:

| Lesson Objective | Teaching Point | Method & Activities | Time | Refs |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 006.01.01 Describe DND units, associated organizations, and policies | Describe current cyber units and their functions. Describe organizations and personnel engaged in management and security of DND networks such as:  the communications and electronics (C&E) branch, units, trades, and positions;  other government organizations (e.g., Director Information Management Engineering and Integration [DIMEI], Shared Services Canada [SSC], Communications Security Establishment [CSE], Defence Research and Development Canada [DRDC], Royal Canadian Mounted Police [RCMP]);  Describe the role of signals in CAF joint operations. | Interactive lecture | 1 x 50 mins |  |
| 006.01.02 Describe DND security orders and directives | Describe DND security orders and directives involving, but not limited to:  physical security;  personnel security;  procedural security; and  etc. | Interactive lecture | 3 x 50 mins |  |
| 006.01.03 Describe security clearance process | Identify clearance levels, to include:  explain security clearance levels; and  explain “need to know”. | Interactive lecture | 1 x 50 mins |  |
| 006.01.04 Manage classified material. | Explain restricting access to classified information, to include:  Sensitive Compartment Information Facility (SCIF), to include: describing threat risk assessment (TRA) process; describing access control and zoning; defining and contrasting SCIF and sensitive discussion area; sanitizing workspace for access; and describing SIGINT facility.  Explain applying classification markings, to include:  determining what to classify;  classifying information according to injury test;  describe second party classification;  applying distribution and classification control markings to documents;  identifying second party agencies and FVEYS agreements; and  recognizing FVEYS security marking equivalencies.  Explain destroying classified material, to include:  applying retention and disposal procedures; and  applying emergency destruction plan and procedures.  Describe open shelf storage, to include:  conducting muster of classified and sensitive material;  operating security containers and locks; and  operating electronic protection and security systems. | Interactive lecture | 6 x 50 mins | A3, Chapter 6, Sections 4.31-4.33, 4.35, 4.36, and Table 4, Chapter 5 Standard A, I, J Section 4.6 A3 Chapter 6 Table 4 4.2, 4.4, and 4.5 A3 Chapter 5 Standard G, H, I, J D33 Section 4.3.1 D33 Sections 7.1.2-7.1.5 D18 D33 Chapter 2,8 D19 |

EO 006.02 Identify DND Network Architecture

- Performance: Identify DND network architecture.

- Conditions:

- given:

- supervision;

- analyst workstation;

- applicable software;

- applicable network traffic;

- applicable network connections; and

- applicable network defence tools;

- denied:

- artificial intelligence;

- assistance;

- unapproved tools/equipment; and

- unapproved scripts;

- environment: classroom environment.

- Standard: IAW specified references, the student shall identify DND network architecture by:

- describing DND network architecture;

- reviewing terminal commands; and

- identifying DND network devices.

- Time Required:

- EO - 40 x 50 min periods; 

- EC - 2 x 60 min period; 

- EC Review - 1 x 50 min period;

