| Lesson Objective | Teaching Point | Method & Activities | Time | Refs |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 007.03.01 Describe cyber threat actors and common cyber kill chains. | Describe the following cyber threat actors:   script kiddies;   ideologies and hacktivists;   terrorists;   criminal organizations;   nation states; and   insider threats Describe the Cyber Kill Chain; and Describe other common frameworks for the cyber kill chain. | Interactive Lecture/ guided discussion | 3 x 50 mins | [https://era.library.ualberta.ca/items/ada5c209-9f7c-4406-bddb-656821859523/view/2ba00c1e-f7a2-4d86-8cb8-c1d70e62cdc2/Sharma_2020_Fall_MISSM.pdf](https://era.library.ualberta.ca/items/ada5c209-9f7c-4406-bddb-656821859523/view/2ba00c1e-f7a2-4d86-8cb8-c1d70e62cdc2/Sharma_2020_Fall_MISSM.pdf) |
| 007.03.02 Briefing Emerging Cyber Threats | Students will brief emerging Cyber Threats by:  Researching about assigned Cyber Threats on the Internet; and   Build and brief to the class/staff their findings. | Practical exercise | 8 x 50 mins |  |
| 007.03.03 Introduction to course technical report | Introduce the sections of the course reporting template with the students and explain the different sections. | Interactive lecture | 1 x 50 mins |  |
| 007.03.04 Report on Network analysis | Students are to draft a technical report involving:  recreate the sequence of events related to the malicious activity;   analysis of the events and the sequence of events;   classify severity;   provide an indication and warning of compromise;   provide source of information;   determine location of affected assets; and   recommend a mitigation strategy. Students are to write technical reports for specified practical exercises in other EOs. | Practical exercises | 12 x 50 mins |  |
| 007.03.05 Brief Technical Report | Communicate findings by:  debrief sequence of events;   analysis of events;   recommend mitigations Students are to brief a technical report to the staff. | Practical Exercise | 4 x 50 mins |  |

## PO 008 Triage Potential Malware

EO 008.01 Conduct Basic Static Analysis on Binaries

- Performance: Conduct basic static analysis on binaries.

- Conditions:

- given:

- supervision;

- analyst workstation;

- applicable software;

- internet connection;

- applicable network connections;

- applicable malware samples; and

- applicable network defence tools.

- denied:

- artificial intelligence;

- assistance;

- unapproved tools/equipment; and

- unapproved scripts.

- environment: computer labs with access to CSTE(Z) or unclassified internet.

- Standard: IAW specified references, the student shall conduct basic static analysis on binaries by:

- describing malicious file types;

- setting up analysis environment;

- hashing binaries; and

- analyzing static behaviour of binaries.

- Time Required:

- EO - 27 x 50 min periods;

- EC - 1 x 60 min period; and

- EC Review - 1 x 50 min period.

- Instructional Methods:

- interactive lecture;

- guided discussions;

- demonstration; and

- practical exercises.

- Training Aids:

- display connected to instructor workstation;

- whiteboard, with markers and eraser;

- PowerPoint slides containing key information related to lessons (e.g., definitions, diagrams, etc.);

- simulated enterprise network;

- applicable binaries;

- malware analysis software;

- packet analysis software; and

- various scripting engines.

- Test Details: See Annex E, Assessment Plan.

- Remarks: Nil.

- Teaching Points:

| Lesson Objective | Teaching Point | Method & Activities | Time | Refs |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 008.01.01  Describe PEs in Windows OS | Triage malware requires a solid base of Windows OS knowledge and how binaries interact with the system. PE format; types of processes; user mode vs kernel mode; loading PEs; dynamic linked library (DLL); filesystems; registry; Windows APIs; and Networking APIs. | Interactive lecture | 4 x 50 mins |  |
| 008.01.02 Describe malicious files | Describe malicious files, to include: identifying types of malwares (e.g., stagers, loaders, RATs, Trojans, Rootkits, spyware, scareware, adware, etc.); describing malicious file behavior (e.g., Dynamic Linked Library injection, process injection, data exfiltration, system monitoring, remote code execution, etc.); and describing anti-forensics behavior, to include: obfuscation techniques; evasion techniques; anti-sandboxing techniques; and anti-analysis techniques. | Interactive lecture Guided Discussion | 4 x 50 mins | C1 (Ch 13) C40 (Ch 1) C136 (Ch1-4) C138 (Ch 8) C142 (Ch 1-2) C148 (Ch 10) |
| 008.01.03 Set up analysis environment | Students will set up and configure their own analysis environment to analyze malware in a secure sandbox from the following provided VMs: malware analysis VM; and network simulation VM. Students will be coached throughout the process to ensure that their analysis environment is configured properly. | Practical Exercise | 2 x 50 mins | C40 (Ch 2-4) |
| 008.01.04 Describe automated analysis tools | Describe the automated analytical tools such as: AVs; Virus total; and Etc. Describe the limitations of automated tools. | Interactive lecture | 1 x 50 mins | C40 (Ch 2-4) C142 |
| 008.01.05 Hash binaries | Students should be able to: describe what hashing is; utilize tools to hash binaries; conduct fuzzy hashing. | Interactive lecture Practical Exercises | 4 x 50 mins | C40 (Ch 2-4) C142 |
| 008.01.06 Analyze static information of binaries | Students will utilize static analysis tools to procure the following information: strings; PE file sections; file metadata; etc. Students will take information generated by previous Teaching Points (TPts) and the tools to analyze behaviour of the binary. Students will categorize and report suspicious indicators. | Interactive lecture Practical exercises | 12 x 50 mins | C40 (Ch 2-4) C142 |

