| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 010.01.01 Describe relations between disciplines | Describe relations between disciplines: network to malware and vice versa; network to logs and vice versa; and malware to logs and vice versa. | Guided Discussion | 1 x 50 mins |  |
| 010.01.02 Reconstruct malicious activity | Students are provided a series of cyber scenarios in which they are expected to analyze and reconstruct any malicious activity found and record in a technical report which includes network, device logs, and malware. Students will prep their workstations after each scenario and prior to the final assessment under instructor guidance. | Practical Exercises | 45 x 50 mins |  |

EO 010.02 Produce a Complete Technical Report

- Performance: Produce a complete technical report.

- Conditions:

- given:

- supervision;

- analyst workstation;

- applicable software;

- internet connection;

- applicable network traffic;

- applicable logs;

- applicable potential malware;

- applicable network connections; and

- applicable network defence hardware.

- denied:

- artificial intelligence;

- assistance;

- unapproved tools/equipment; and

- unapproved scripts.

- environment: computer labs with access to CSTE(Z) or unclassified internet.

- Standard: IAW specified references, the student shall produce a complete technical report by:

- recreating the sequence of events related to the malicious activity;

- conducting an analysis of the events and the sequence of events;

- classifying severity;

- providing an indication and warning of compromise;

- providing source of information;

- determining location of affected assets; and

- recommending a mitigation strategy;

- Time Required:

- EO - 6 x 50 min periods;

- PC brief - 1 x 50 min periods;

- PC 010 - 16 x 60 min period;

- PC Review - 1 x 50 min period;

- PC Remedial - 6 x 50 min period;

- PC Retest - 16 x 60 min period; and

- PC Retest Review - 1 x 50 min period.

- Instructional Methods: practical exercises.

- Training Aids:

- display connected to instructor workstation;

- whiteboard, with markers and eraser;

- PowerPoint slides containing key information related to lessons (e.g., definitions, diagrams, etc.);

- simulated enterprise network;

- relevant network traffic;

- relevant logs;

- relevant malicious binaries;

- packet analysis software;

- binary analysis software; and

- various scripting engines.

- Test Details: See Annex E, Assessment Plan.

- Remark: Nil.

- Teaching Points:

| Lesson Objective | Teaching Point | Method & Activities | Time | Refs |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 010.02.01 Produce a complete Technical Report | Students are to draft a technical report involving: recreate the sequence of events related to the malicious activity; analysis of the events and the sequence of events; classify severity; provide an indication and warning of compromise; provide source of information; determine location of affected assets; recommend a mitigation strategy; name of binary; severity rating; 3 different hashes including a fuzzy hash; summary of the binary; evidence discovered from static and dynamic analysis; and analysis of the binary behaviour. | Practical exercises | 6 x 50 mins |  |

