| SME | De Boer | J | Capt | CFSCE, Kingston |
| SME | Hicks | K | Capt | CFNOC, Ottawa |
| SME | Tobias | K | Capt | CFNOC, Ottawa |
| SME | Phinney | G | WO | CFNOC, Ottawa |
| SME | Riding | J | Sgt | CFSCE, Kingston |
| SME | Inverarity | J | MS | CFNOC, Ottawa |
| SME | Cadieux | R | MCpl | CFNOC, Ottawa |
| SME | Tumber | K | LS | CFNOC, Ottawa |
| SME | Smith | R | Dr | RMCC, Kingston |
| SME | Morvan | Y | Mr | DGIMTSP/DIMEI 3, Ottawa |
| SME/Secretary | Oshier | J | Sgt | DPGR 2, Ottawa |
| Advisor | Norman | W | CPO2 | DPGR 2, Ottawa |
| Threat Int SME | Racicot | J | Capt | CFNOC, Ottawa |
| Forensics SME | Grant | N | MCpl | CFNOC, Ottawa |

| **Ser #** | **Details** | **Action** |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | The QSWB was convened at 0830 hours, 13 Feb 17.  The Co-Chair (Content) welcomed the board members and provided some background information on the way ahead for the CYBER OP occupation and associated training.  The Co-Chair (Process) explained the board members’ responsibilities and the expected deliverables for the week (revised tasks, completed QS and detailed RoDs), explained the format of the job-based specification (JBS) and associated master task list, and provided an overview of the Analysis process the board would be following.  The importance of including a high level of detail and identifying pertinent references was stressed. | Nil. |
| **TASK LIST REFINEMENT** |
| 2 | The QSWB reviewed the job description and associated tasks for the Cyber Def Analyst.  Serials 4 – 9 outline discussions that arose, by Duty Area, around some tasks and recommended changes to the task list (Duty Areas D – Standards and Training, and H – Pers Management, do not apply at this rank level). Several typos and misspellings have been documented in the revised task list that will be submitted to DPGR (those minor editorial changes are not documented here).  The following criteria were applied when revising existing tasks or developing new tasks to confirm their validity:  Is the task discrete, independent, and a complete segment of work?  Is the task observable and measurable?  Does the task have an identifiable beginning and end?  Can you “task” someone to do it? Is there a tangible product or definite output after the task is completed? Several of the tasks associated with Cyber Def Analyst also apply to intermediate Pte/Cpl jobs, or jobs at higher rank levels; as such, they are written generically, or in some cases at a level beyond what is expected of the Cyber Def Analyst.  In these cases, the board clarified what the tasks meant at the Pte/Cpl level (i.e., what aspect of the task they will perform, appropriate scope, etc.) when developing the standard statements for the POs to keep too much ‘expertise development’ from being inadvertently included at the introductory/entry level. | D Cyber FD Trg Dev Advisor to staff required amendments to the Occ Authority (DMPPPC 4, for A/CMP) for onward submission to DPGR Spec Maintenance. |
| 3 | When the master task list was refined in the fall of 2015, an effort was made to align the tasks with current industry standards and lexicons/terminology, given that many of the tasks performed by CYBER OPs are performed outside of a military context. Dr. Smith led this discussion, highlighting the importance of adopting standard lexicons (including tasks, skills, and knowledge elements) used by the broader cyber community, particularly the National Initiative for Cybersecurity Education (NICE) Cybersecurity Workforce Framework (NCWF).  The board agreed that employing common terminology to the extent possible was a critical aspect of identifying training requirements, but also of shaping the CAF Cyber Force to maintain pace with other government, corporate and academic organizations. The NCWF was used extensively as a reference by the board. | TPWB |
| 4 | Duty Area A – Policy:  A new task is required related to applying cyber security orders and directives.  The task list contains policy enforcement, development and approval-related tasks associated with higher rank levels but does not address a related requirement at the Pte/Cpl level.  The board recommended that the task “Apply cyber security orders and directives” be added to the task list (“Applies” = places in contact with; puts to use, employees).  This new task builds from task G4.010 from the NCM General Specification, “Apply general and operational security (OPSEC) and procedures”. | As per Ser #2 |
| 5 | The tasks found in Duty Area B (Operations) were revised as follows: BT0280 - Assess extent of identified vulnerabilities:  This task is an aspect of analyzing vulnerability assessment results, a function of the Cyber Def Analyst. (ADD to Cyber Def Analyst task list) BT0350 - Monitor network sensors for cyber events:  The board recommended that this task be revised to "Respond to a cyber event" to better reflect the span of possible actions (original task was too narrowly focused).   The board determined that this reworded task is still appropriate for the Sr Cyber Def Analyst and the Cyber Def Supervisor. (REVISE task statement) BT0360 - Extract cyber indicators and malware samples from network traffic:  This task includes two distinct things that require different methods and processes to get to their end state.  The board recommended that this task be deleted, and two new distinct tasks be added to replace it (“Extract Network Indicators”, and “Extract Artifacts”). (DELETE BT0360; ADD “Extract Network Indicators” and “Extract Artifacts”) BT0390 - Validate network alerts through coordination with enterprise-wide cyber defense staff:  This is a senior function; at the Cyber Def Analyst level they should be validating the alert based on analysis and internal database searches and coordination.  This function was addressed by the QS board as part of responding to a cyber event. (REMOVE from Cyber Def Analyst task list) BT0450 - Extract artifacts for digital forensics analysis:  This task is not performed by the Cyber Def Analyst (responsibility of the Digital Forensics Analyst).  Any support the Cyber Def Analyst might provide to forensics processes is addressed by ET0050 (Collect artifacts). (REMOVE from Cyber Def Analyst task list)  ***Secretarial Note, 8 Apr 18***: During the Threat Intelligence (Int) Analyst Job and Task Verification Board conducted 14-16 Nov 17, it was determined that two tasks originally designated as “no train” in the Pte RQ QS (BT0560 and BT0580) must now be trained. These tasked were to be performed by the Threat Int Analyst; however, this job is to be removed from the CYPER OP JBS as it has been designated as an Int Operator/Int Officer function. The two tasks that must still be performed by CYBER OPs to support either Int or cyber functions can be carried out by the Cyber Defence Analyst. As such, POs 002, 003 and 007 have been amended to address the training requirements for these tasks. These two tasks have been revised as follows: BT0560 - Research real time open-source cyber events for Indications and Warnings (I&W): Researching is a skill that enables identification of indicators of cyber activity (this is the actual end-state, or why the research is conducted). Task revised to “Identify indicators of cyber activity”. (REVISE task statement) BT0580 - Request data collection in support of identified intelligence gaps: Task reworded to “Produce a request for information (RFI)” to more accurately reflect what must be produced to request intelligence collection. (REVISE task statement) | As per Ser #2 |
| 6 | The tasks found in Duty Area C (Reporting) were revised as follows: CT0010 - Inform customers on basic computer network defence (CND) and status updates:  CND is an outdated term; additionally, the board determined that the task was too Helpdesk focused as written ("customer" was too narrowly focused).  The board recommended that the task be reworded to state “Inform on cyber security best practices/policy”.  This revision applies to all jobs at the Pte/Cpl levels. (REVISE task statement) CT0130 – Generate analysis log:  The original intent of this task when it was revised in September 2015 was to reflect the requirement to document actions taken during analyses.  This is not the same as formal system-generated logs; as such, the board recommended rewording this task to "Document analysis notes". (REVISE task statement) CT0180 - Report risks and vulnerabilities to client:  This is an aspect of producing technical reports, a function of the Cyber Def Analyst. (ADD to Cyber Def Analyst task list) CT0190 - Report analysis findings to client: This is an aspect of producing technical reports, a function of the Cyber Def Analyst. (ADD to Cyber Def Analyst task list) | As per Ser #2 |
| 7 | The tasks found in Duty Area E (Collection) were revised as follows: ET0050 – Collect intrusion artifacts (e.g., source code, malware, and trojans):  Task was seen as too narrow as written; artifacts may also be false-positives and not indicators of intrusion. The board recommended that the word "intrusion" be removed from the statement.  This change applies to all jobs performing this task. (REVISE task statement) ET0060 - Capture event logs for analysis:  Original task was seen as too narrow as written (logs are one of several things that will be collected).  The board recommended that the task be revised to “Collect metadata, network traffic and logs”. This change applies to all jobs performing this task. (REVISE task statement) ET0090 - Develop logical network map: This is an element of maintaining SA of the network, a function of the Cyber Def Analyst. (ADD to Cyber Def Analyst task list) | As per Ser #2 |
| 8 | The tasks found in Duty Area F (Tool Set Maintenance) were revised as follows: FT0010 to FT0060 are performed by the majority of intermediate jobs at the Pte/Cpl level; the board determined that Cyber Def Analysts will also perform these tasks as elements of preparing an analysis environment. The board also determined that Senior Cyber Def Analysts will also supervise completion of, or perform, these tasks. (ADD all six tasks to Cyber Def Analyst and Senior Cyber Def Analyst task lists) FT0010 (Install authorized hardware), (FT0020 - Remove authorized hardware), FT0040 (Install authorized software), and FT0050 (Remove authorized software): There will at times be a requirement to install/remove and configure non-authorized software and hardware to mirror an environment seen within DND/CAF.  In order to duplicate an event or issue, the CYBER OP will need (in a controlled environment) to be able to duplicate findings. The board therefore recommended that the word “authorized” be removed from these four tasks.  This change applies to all jobs performing this task. (REVISE task statements) FT0280 - Develop custom network testing toolsets: The Cyber Def Analyst will develop software tools, an aspect of this broader task. (ADD to Cyber Def Analyst task list) FT0290 - Test custom network assessment functionality:  This is an element of maintaining situational awareness of the network, a function of the Cyber Def Analyst. (ADD to Cyber Def Analyst task list) FT0340 - Build custom testing environment:  The scope of this task in the Cyber Def Analyst context involves preparing an environment to analyze cyber events; in essence, they are building testing environments.  The Senior Cyber Def Analyst will also be required to do this. (ADD to Cyber Def Analyst and Senior Cyber Def Analyst task lists) The board also recommended that this task applies to Red and Blue Analysts, Snr Blue Analyst, and Blue Supr as both teams will need to build custom environments to test equipment and configurations, and to duplicate or mirror an existing event or issue. (ADD to Red and Blue Analyst, Snr Blue Analyst, and Blue Supr task lists) | As per Ser #2 |
| 9 | The tasks found in Duty Area G (Analysis) were revised as follows: GT0040 - Analyze identified malicious activity to determine weaknesses exploited:  Task is awkwardly worded; board recommended “Identify means of compromise” as a clearer alternative (completed via command-and-control channels and exfiltration and supports accomplishment of task BT0420 - Reconstruct a malicious activity based on network traffic). (REVISE task statement) GT0080 - Analyze network traffic to identify anomalous activity and potential threats to network resources:  Traffic is analyzed for many things - identifying anomalous activity and threats are only two.  The board determined that the task is too narrow as written (these two things are sub-tasks of "Analyze network traffic"). (REVISE task statement) GT0250 – Analyze network sensor data for cyber event activity:  IDS alerts come from multiple sources of alert generation - they are not only trying to get data from a network sensor.  As such, the board recommended that task be revised to “Analyze IDS alerts”. This change applies to all jobs performing this task. (REVISE task statement) | As per Ser #2 |
| **QUALIFICATION STANDARD DEVELOPMENT** |
| 10 | The board began their analysis by completing an exercise to identify the broader functional “work areas” and the tasks associated with those functions.  Once this was completed, the board reviewed the task list for Cyber Def Analyst and determined that all tasks associated with this entry level job require training as they are new to the target population, are complex/important, etc.  Training priorities were also assigned to the “train” tasks (see Annex B, Task Disposition). The following training priority (TP) level descriptions were applied:  High TP:  over-train to ensure retention and to minimize the likelihood of deterioration of performance or skill fade over time.  Moderate TP:  instruction up to the level required to do the task competently, assuming that task performance follows soon after completion of the instructional programme. Low TP:  learner needs a general familiarity with the task and will become proficient on the job. | TPWB |
| 11 | Scalar development began on Day 2 (14 Feb 17) and continued until Day 5 (17 Feb 17).  The board focused on how each task and task grouping related to others, and sequencing PO components in a logical order (e.g., in performance order, simple to complex, theory to practical application, generic to specific).  The board was cognizant of the fact that the scope of each PO must be manageable from a teaching and learning perspective and “assessable”, in that a realistic, suitably scoped performance check could be developed for it.  Once tasks were organized and grouped into seven POs, each task was further analyzed to determine any sub-tasks, steps in processes, etc.  A number of tasks appear in more than one PO, given that they apply to different aspects of the job, or are carried out in different contexts.  The board clearly defined what the tasks entail each time they appeared in a PO. | TPWB |
| 12 | As scalar development progressed, concerns were raised regarding a number of tasks not directly related to the Cyber Def Analyst job being included in the POs (could lead to over-training, making the training too long, expanding the scope of the job beyond the original intent, etc.).  Some of these tasks were identified as part of the job in the task list (but were seen as being too complex, performed at a higher level, etc., as written) or were seen by the board as being directly linked to other tasks, but not identified in the task list as part of the Cyber Def Analyst job. The board ultimately came to the consensus that aspects of a Cyber Def Analyst’s job touch on other Pte/Cpl level jobs (e.g., their performing action ‘X’ contributes to activity ‘Y’ that is a primary responsibility of another job).  The board clearly defined within the POs the scope of these tasks for the Cyber Def Analyst, highlighting which element of a broader, or highly complex, task that they would be responsible for. | TPWB to be cognizant of possibility of “scope creep” when designing the TP. |
| 13 | Task BT0210 - Transfer customer enquiries to appropriate sections:  This task was interpreted to mean transferring different clients and their enquiries, or escalating issues, within a unit. This task was created in response to the requirements of a Cyber Operations Center’s Tier 1 analyst position, which also includes network monitoring. | TPWB |
| 14 | Discussion was held on tasks BT0410 (Identify device applications and operating systems based on network traffic) and BT0420 (Reconstruct a malicious activity based on network traffic).  They were seen as too specific due to the inclusion of “network traffic” in the statements, as one could identify and reconstruct applications, OSs, and malicious activity based on a number of things.  These tasks were interpreted as such in the POs (i.e., broader than the tasks as written). | TPWB |
| 15 | On the morning of Day 6, Comd CFIOG and representatives from CFNOC (DCO, RSM, OC B Sqn) were briefed on the board’s progress to date and were given a high-level overview of the proposed POs.  Initial feedback was positive, with no changes in focus or direction required. Days 6 to 10 (20-24 Feb 17) were dedicated to writing POs, identifying relevant reference material, cross-referencing the POs with the task list, documenting required amendments to the master task list, and finalizing the QS. | Nil. |
| 16 | The vast majority of references cited in the POs are not DND/CAF or allied partner documents, but open source or commercial publications, documents and/or resources. The technical nature of the work currently necessitates this (and most likely will continue to be the case going forward).  Additionally, some source references for POs 006 and 007 are not available at the UNCLAS level. Notes have been added to Annex A (References) identifying how to directly obtain CLASSIFED reference material from other sources. | TPWB |
| 17 | PO 001, Maintain network situational awareness:  The intent of this PO is to prepare the Cyber Def Analyst to conduct activities to establish and maintain situational awareness of the structure, composition, traffic patterns and security posture of a network. This is achieved through use of automated tools, manual methods, and various levels of analysis of the raw data. Though this PO focuses on preparing the Cyber Def Analyst to maintain situational awareness of a network for the purposes of training and assessment, it is expected that they will be able to transfer acquired skills and knowledge to maintaining situational awareness of the cyber domain (defined as “all infrastructure, entities, users and activities related to, or affecting, cyberspace”).  This broader responsibility must be clearly articulated in the TP. General knowledge requirements:  In enumerating the network, building the network map and benchmarking network traffic, the Cyber Def Analyst must be well-versed in network technologies, architecture, devices and communications.  In conducting vulnerability assessments, they must be knowledgeable of common vulnerabilities, threats, and attack vectors in order to analyze and advise on mitigations.  Additionally, they must have a general understanding of various operating systems (PC and server), virtualization and various other areas of networking and IT, in order to identify and assess if any anomalies were detected during a network scan or in packet captures. | TPWB |
| 18 | PO 002, Respond to a cyber event:  The intent of this PO is to prepare the Cyber Def Analyst to investigate an event using data collected from a variety of cyber defence tools (e.g., IDS alerts, firewalls, network traffic logs).  The analyst will go through the steps to analyze the event that occurred within their environment for the purposes of mitigating threats through internal reporting. | TPWB |
| 19 | PO 003, Produce a technical report:  The intent of this PO is to prepare the Cyber Def Analyst to produce a technical report using any and all raw available data, completed analyses and other relevant reports and cyber injects. | TPWB |
| 20 | PO 004, Prepare an analysis environment:  This PO prepares the Cyber Def Analyst to configure hardware and software.  The analyst will be capable of installing, maintaining and removing cyber defence hardware and software and creating and removing IDS/IPS rules.  The setup and configuration of various security devices in a small network environment will prepare the analyst for custom applications and unique deployment requirements. General skill and knowledge requirements:  In support of CAF forensics and malware analysis, the Cyber Def Analyst requires a hands-on understanding a virtualized platform. | TPWB |
| 21 | PO 005, Develop software tools:  This PO prepares the Cyber Def Analyst to design, build, test, and maintain custom software tools.  They will be able to determine deficiencies in existing tools in order to design and build tools to resolve those deficiencies.  They will have competency in scripting and be able to write small programs for the purpose of building custom tools.  This PO is not intended to make Cyber Def Analysts expert at software development but will give them basic scripting skills and provide foundational skills for other POs as well as future specialized job-based training. FT0280, Develop custom network analysis toolsets, was determined to be too restrictive by limiting the task to network analysis.  Broadening the scope to “software tools” includes foundational skills/knowledge required for future specialized roles (Blue Analyst, Red Analyst, Digital Forensics Analyst, Cyber Support Analyst, and Threat Intelligence Analyst). | TPWB |
| 22 | PO 006, Apply cyber security orders and directives:  This PO will prepare the Cyber Def Analyst to appropriately mark material, ensure the physical security of equipment/infrastructure, handle classified materials, and apply SIGINT/cyber policies to cyber defence activities. The PO on policy and security found within the Communicator Research (Comm Rsch) QL3 (ABBA) QS is similar to this PO and should be referred to when developing the training plan. | TPWB |
| 23 | PO 007, Defend the DND/CAF cyber domain: Though this PO prepares the Cyber Def Analyst to defend the DND/CAF cyber domain, for the purposes of training and assessment, students will be required to demonstrate that they can defend a DND/CAF network.  It is then expected that they be able to transfer acquired skills and knowledge to defence of the broader DND/CAF cyber domain. This broader responsibility must be clearly articulated in the training plan TP. The intent of this PO is to have the Cyber Def Analyst apply the generic skills and network monitoring techniques developed in POs 001–005 in a CAF context. Using tools, networks, and tradecraft specific to the CAF, the analyst will monitor and defend a DND/CAF network. Examples include using the reporting database, SIEM, Arcsite, etc.  Employing in-service tools, the Cyber Def Analyst will work through the identification of an event through to referral for additional analysis, or to its completion and finalization through reporting (path dependent upon the specific event and complexity). The majority of training associated with this PO must be conducted at the SECRET level; the threat intelligence aspect must be conducted at the TOP SECRET (POST-BOARD AMENDMENT: Has been downgraded to SECRET) level. | TPWB |
| 24 | FT0070, Coordinate software licensing:  Discussion of this task arose following inclusion of tasks FT0010-FT0060 in the Cyber Def Analyst and Snr Cyber Def Analyst task lists. A question was raised whether inclusion of these tasks for these jobs now requires someone (e.g., Cyber Def Supervisor) to coordinate possible software licencing issues.  In reality, a civilian employee would likely be responsible for commercial licences for any given unit. However, the task of coordination could be as simple as the Cyber Def Supervisor ensuring that the civilian employee has the necessary information and that current licences have been applied to their job-specific software applications. | Discussion to be deferred to the MCpl and Sgt RQ boards. |
| **TRAINING-RELATED DISCUSSIONS** |
| 25 | The board developed a detailed list of knowledge elements for each PO (based in part on the task, skill and knowledge lists included in the National Initiative for Cyber Education (NICE) Cybersecurity Workforce Framework (NCWF)). It was stressed that foundational knowledge be linked directly to either a specific task from the job-based specification, or to a “sub-task” included as part of a PO. | D Cyber FD Trg Dev Advisor to provide list to CFSCE/TPWB Co-chairs. |
| 26 | In support of custom toolset creation/maintenance and to the benefit of Red, Blue, Forensics and Cyber Def Analyst roles (especially TAV events), the inclusion of programming languages in Pte/Cpl RQ training was discussed.  The board determined that an understanding of, and basic ability to use, programming languages will be a requirement of the Cyber Def Analyst in support of this and other jobs. | TPWB |
| 27 | The overall delivery strategy for Pte/Cpl RQ training was frequently discussed during the board.  The challenges of training CYBER OPs at CFSCE in a highly technical field that is constantly evolving were discussed.  It is recognized by the cyber community that successful performance in this domain requires a substantial educational foundation, in additional to CAF-specific training.  Given how rapidly technology and best practices change in this field, it is recognized that colleges are best positioned to keep their course content current, given their ability to employ experts in the domain, and to dedicate substantial resources to research and development. The CAF training system would be challenged to equal what the colleges can currently provide, given the limited availability of SMEs and the challenges involved in keeping course documentation and content up to date.  With this in mind, the board developed the QS to facilitate potentially delivery of POs 001 – 005 by a college or similar educational institution and POs 006 and 007 by a CAF training establishment. | D Cyber FD staff to liaise with MILPERSGEN HQ to investigate inclusion of CYBER OP in the Non-Commissioned Member Subsidized Training and Education Plan (NCMSTEP) and the CAF Accreditation, Certification and Equivalencies (CAF-ACE) directory. |
| **TRAINING PLAN DEVELOPMENT** |
| 28 | Training plan development (Chapters 3 and 4) occurred over several months from September 2017 to May 2018 for PO 006 – Apply cyber security orders and directives (see serial 29 below) and PO 007 – Defend the DND/CAF Cyber Domain (see serials 30 – 34 below). | Nil. |
| 29 | PO 006, Apply cyber security orders and directives: Two EOs were developed for this PO based on what is currently included in COMM RSCH DP1 training on similar topics. Classified content related to these EOs is not included in this QSP (held on CFSCE’s classified networks). | CFSCE courseware developers |
| 30 | EO 007.01 - Describe DND networks, policies, and defensive architecture: The board recommended that a significant amount of time must be spent familiarizing students with CAF networks, personnel, and organizations as they will have no prior experience with the aforementioned subjects. Students must understand the networks that they are defending, how they are used, their importance to the CAF, and the people who use and administrate the networks. EO 007.01 was therefore created to ensure students understand the context in which they will be operating. Students are required to be familiar with the subject matter (i.e., be able to describe each aspect) but are not expected to master it at this stage in their career. The EC for this EO is therefore formative in nature (see Assessment Plan, Annex E, EC #1). | CFSCE courseware developers |
| 31 | EO 007.02 (Categorize normal DND network traffic), EO 007.03 (Categorize malicious activity), EO 007.04 (Investigate cyber events), and EO 007.05 (Categorize software binaries): The functions described in these EOs make up the core of the CYBER OP job; as such, the majority of the time needed to deliver training for PO 007 is allocated here. In order to respond to a cyber event a CYBER OP must have a detailed understanding of methods, tools, tactics, and practices employed by a wide variety of threat actors. As such, emphasis is to be placed on understanding offensive cyber operations (OCO). The focus of these EOs is on applying the foundational education and training related to detecting threat activity and responding to cyber events gained through POs 001–005 to the unique problem sets face by the CAF. These EOs will be assessed through a critical enabling check (EC) (see Assessment Plan, Annex E, EC #2) following numerous practice opportunities involving regular student debriefs on their performance, labs, and exercises to ensure comprehension and retention of difficult subject matter. For practice, students will be expected to fill out a report template for each lab and exercise. They will be assessed on the content of their reports but not the format, style, presentation, or quality of writing at this stage. | CFSCE courseware developers |
| 32 | EO 007.06 - Report on an emerging cyber threat: The board identified two key styles of reporting required by CYBER OPs at DP1: summiting a technical report on a cyber incident to a supervisor and presenting technical information about a current cyber event to personnel without a technical background. In order to assess a student’s ability to present a report on a cyber event, practice and testing scenarios must be realistic and similar to those they will have seen during EOs 007.02 – 007.05. Students should be assessed on the technical content of the report and the quality of their writing (e.g., accuracy, relevance, clarity) (see Assessment Plan, Annex E, EC #3 Part 1). It was noted in order to recommend mitigation strategy and changes to a defensive posture, students must have a working knowledge of how to implement these specific changes. The board determined that CYBER OPs must be able to provide technical briefings comprehendible by personnel without technical backgrounds. Students will therefore be assessed on how effectively they convey information to a non-technical audience (see Assessment Plan, Annex E, EC #3 Part 2). | CFSCE courseware developers |
| 33 | EO 007.07 - Safeguard forensic evidence: The purpose of this EO is to ensure that CYBER OPs are able to safely capture forensic evidence for digital forensic investigators. They will not, however, be required to perform forensic investigation. | CFSCE courseware developers |
| 34 | The board discussed the following in relation to the PC for PO 007: The board identified two distinct scenarios/contexts that a DP1 CYBER OP must be capable of operating in based on the unique environments in which they can be employed: operating in a NOC environment at the national level (e.g., CFNOC) and operating in a NOC environment as part of a deployed task force. These two environments have some significant differences related to types of traffic, services, and the demands of the network; as such, it is essential to test a CYBER OP under both sets of conditions. It was determined that the most effective way to evaluate a student’s ability to defend the DND/CAF Cyber domain is through two final exercises simulating the two environments (see Assessment Plan, Annex E, PC 007 Parts 1 and 2). (POST-BOARD AMMENDMENT: obtaining internal-DWAN traffic unfeasible.) Students must be assessed on their ability to perform all tasks in a team under the supervision of a MCpl/MS. As such, they will be assessed on their individual performance and their performance as a member of a team. | CFSCE courseware developers/Stds staff |

