| 13 | The co-chair (Process) reviewed the RoD with the board mbrs and minor editorial changes were made.  EO 007.03 was deemed a Critical EC (CEC) as it is an integral part of the course for students to understand networking fundamentals that may contain malicious activity/traffic prior to moving forward with more difficult aspect of the course.  The board discussed the prospect of making EO 007.04 a CEC as well. After a lengthy discussion the board came to the consensus to keep this as a regular EC. EO 007.04 title was changed from “Categorize software binaries “to Triage Potential Malware” to better define the intent of the learning objectives.  Potential malware may include a wide variety of artifacts such as compiled binaries, scripts, macro documents, PDF JavaScript and other files containing exploits. | Nil |
| 14 | EO 007.05 was deemed a Critical EC (CEC) as this is a new section that categorizes malicious activity on hosts. This is a prerequisite in order to move forward with the remainder of the course and is crucial for students to understand the fundamentals to successful be employed as a Cyber Operator. EO 007.03.05 will consist of a written test questions/answers test to cover these ECs and have added reporting/timeline in order to give the students ample practice for briefing the CoC.  EO 007.06To be taught early in the schedule potentially after the tool refresher lecture. Student needs learn how to brief their findings and convey the correct timeline of events. EO 007.03 Students will be required to write a report and produce a timeline but will not be formally assessed during this EO. EO 007.10 (Host) was moved after EO 007.07 (Forensics) EO 007.07(Safeguard forensic evidence/Collect artifacts), added conducting forensic triage. Board discussion expanded into how much of forensics needs to be included in this EO.  Students will be required to collect appropriate artifacts for triage from the forensics image. Scoring will be focused on the successful duplication and preservation of the original image. | Nil |
| 15 | EO 007.08 This is an exercise where students will be given a network that was attacked and reconstruct the timeline of the attack. They will be retested once! Had a visit from DGICFD leadership Maj Holtz and MWO Phinney.  The board discussed the possibility of rewriting a completely new Pte RQ to Sgt RQ course and to also conduct a review of the JBS to update the Master Task List (MTL) as some of the tasks do not currently reflect what a Cyber Operator should do based on current industry standards. The path from DP1 to DP2 was asked, how many years of OJE/OJT should there be between the two quals? Should there be USQs or Delta training in between the two quals to bridge any gaps that may hinder our operators to perform effectively. The board were told due to the Cyber Officer study there has been a halt in approving the DP2 as it will have effect on how NCMs training is delivered in the future. A decision is forthcoming rather sooner than later. The board proposed the option of having new recruits to attend accredited colleges and obtain diploma options, or private contract with industries that could help better train/shape the fundamentals of a Cyber Operator prior to attending the Pte RQ crse. Potential Cyber Officer trade still in review and research which may help shape the requirements for the Cyber Operator trade. PO 007.08 days accounted for preparation, execution, debriefs, and retest. Removed team-based wording. The PC is individual testing there is no team assessment. There will be a small/short brief at the end of the PO which ensure the students is along a sound path and avoiding rabbit holes. | Nil |
| 16 | End of day three, board chairs summarized today’s activity, and a timing of 0900 was given for the following day. | Nil |
|  | **Day 4 - 09 Dec 21** |  |
| 17 | The co-chair (Process) reviewed the RoD with the board mbrs and minor editorial changes were made.  A final count of periods from all EOs of PO 006 and 007 was accounted for.  Additional periods were redistributed between retests.  Annex E - Assessments Plan was reviewed and depicted below: EC #1 006.04 Multiple Choice questions, no short answers. EC #2 No change. CEC #3 increased from 4 periods to 8 periods this is to include review and retesting. EC #4 007.04 (Triage potential malware) changed the scoring for the EC. 4 points per artifact and 2 points for a logical explanation/indicator. CEC #5 Categorizing malicious host activity. Went from 4 to 8 periods to account for retest and added the report writing, timeline creation. The report is non-critical. One additional day of remedial and practice students will then be retested. Failure to pass the retest will result in a PRB IAW TE SOs. CEC 7.03 - 7.05 added non-critical reporting EC #7 Safeguard forensic evidence. EC #8 007.10 No change to the PCs | Nil |
| 18 | End of day four, board chairs summarized today’s activity, and a timing of 1000 was given for the following day. | Nil |
|  | **Day 5 - 10 Dec 21** |  |
| 19 | The co-chair (Process) reviewed the RoD with the board mbrs and minor editorial changes were made.  The DP1 board wants to clarify that the DP1 is a DP1. Graduates are not expected to be able to perform the job assigned to a Cyber Operator without extensive and consistent on-the-job supervision. Graduates will not progress to the DP2 level without skilled mentorship and guidance. DP1 graduates should not be looked at as a solution to CAF cyber’s problems. DP1 graduates should never be considered as subject matter experts. This DP1 board was convened to “ensure our uniformed Cyber Operators are positioned in a few years to onboard cloud security monitoring and analysis”. DP1 graduates will not be able to onboard cloud monitoring unless the existing cyber operators doing the job dedicate themselves to technical competence in order to provide the required mentorship. We recommend that a cyber-training cell be developed within the force employer in order to deliver flexible position-specific training. |  |
| **Post-Board Activities** |
| 20 | A thorough reviewed of Annex A - References, will be conducted by the CFSCE instructional staff to organize and verify their accuracies. The vast majority of references cited in the POs are not DND/CAF or allied partner documents, but open source or commercial publications, documents and/or resources as the technical nature of the work currently necessitates this. This will be achieved by reviewing the MLPs against references currently utilized to build the courseware. | H-tp instructional staff |
| 21 | The following new tasks were added during the review board and will need to be updated in the JBS. Categorize normal DND network traffic Categorize malicious activity in network traffic Triage potential malware Categorize malicious activity on hosts Investigate Cyber events Conduct vulnerability analysis and exploitation Categorize malicious activity in the cloud | D Cyber FD to staff required new tasks to DPGR Spec Maintenance for updates |

**RECORD OF AMENDMENTS**

### Version 1.0

| **Record of amendment – ****Version ****1.0** |
| --- |
| Date | Location |
| Jan 2023 | CFSCE, Kingston |
| **Requirement for Amendment****:** This QSP has been amended to reflect the new changes to TP. |
| **Description of change (s)****: **The following changes have been made to this QSP: Move 7.1 (DND Arch.) to PO 006 To keep all Lvl II content together; content is closely related as well Reassign EO #s accordingly ** Note: order of EO delivery at Crse Dir discretion based on your AAR with DS 7.01 – Normal traffic 7.02 – Malicious network traffic analysis 7.03 – Malware 7.04 – Malicious host log analysis 7.05 – Reporting 7.06 – Collect artifacts 7.07 – Investigate cyber events 7.08 – Exploitation 7.09 – Cloud  Remove CEC (7.2-7.5) No longer required since Net and Host ECs are now critical The following are the key changes being requested to the TP: Increase number of resourced training days to Mod 3 (121794) by four days in order to allowed for more graduation parade preparation time - two days will be taken from Mod 2 (121793), which will be indicated in a separate TP, which yields a net gain of only two days. Substantiation: The course is heavily dense with technical content and not enough time is allocated for graduation parade administration and practices. An extra two training days will alleviate stress on staff and resources. Moreover, if PO 007 were to be the last portion of the course before graduation, the grad parade would be the day after the PC retest in accordance with the current schedule, whereas most courses at CFSCE have several days in between their final ex/test and the grad parade. Update resources required to conduct courses of 24 students and reflect a more appropriate staff to student ratio; including creating teaching assistant positions. Substantiation: The surge of Cyber Operators and increase of SIP yields a requirement to double the output; thereby increasing the student max load. This also comes with staffing implications as well as the requirement teaching assistants in the classroom to support the lead instructor of a particular EO. Remove EO 007.01 (121794); Add to EO 006.03 (121793) Substantiation: EO 007.01 (121794) is classified content and makes more sense to be coupled with the classified PO, i.e. PO 006 (121793). Update assessment plan to make EO 007.02 (Network) and EO 007.04 (Logs) have critical EC evaluations; follow on effect, remove the cumulative critical EC that covers EO 007.01 to EO 007.04 Substantiation: The board that sat in Dec 2021 indicated a strong need to increase the technical difficulty of two key EC assessments and to make them critical ECs, thereby negating the relevancy of the original cumulative critical EC that covers EO 007.01 to EO 007.04. Add two new EO - E0 007.08 Exploitation Techniques and EO 007.09 Cloud Security Substantiation: The addition of two new EOs (Exploitation Techniques and Cloud Security) were indicated by a letter by Comd CFIOG and the TP board of Dec 2021; with the cloud being a "newer" vector space of exploitation, it is a space that needs to be defended against; also, a good defender needs to also see the "attacker" perspective so that they can defend more effectively ergo the need for teaching exploitation techniques was introduced. Update EO 007.04 (Logs) teaching points to be more relevant to the emerging threats of modern cyberspace. Substantiation: The teaching points for log analysis never got updated from the TP board that sat in Dec 2021 - the content will be updated so that the dev team can update the current content. The new TP will include the following additions: EO 007.04: Categorize malicious activity on host Explaining and understanding operating system internals to include: User Mode vs Kernal Mode DLLs Filesystems Registry Windows APIs Networking APIs Windows processes related to threat hunting:   System   SMSS   CSRSS   WININIT   Services   LSASS   svchost   LSM   WinLogon   UserInit   Explorer  Explaining and understanding Windows Events: Types Sources Categories Identifiers Forwarding Windows Events Explaining and demonstrating sysmon: Sysmon logs and analysis Logging Architectures Sysmon configuration using XML to filter and review events Sysmon and how it relates to:   Active Directory   User Groups   Permissions / Shares   PowerShell Categorizing malicious host log activity includes: Use of malicious PowerShell (ps), such as PS Core, Empire PS, and Powersploit Administrative privilege abuse File system abuse Operating System abuse   Process injection   Process hollowing   Process migration   Service configuration Application abuse Persistence mechanisms Internal reconnaissance Privilege escalation   Credential theft   Bypass UAC   Access token manipulation   Misconfiguration exploitation Network access Access control abuse Configure host logging environment, such as sysmon Ingest data into a SIEM, such as the Elasticsearch Logstash Kibana (ELK) stack, and utilize visualization and exploration tools to analyze malicious activity and report on the event Impact: Minimal impact as majority changes is from TP review board in Dec 2021. Overall trg day increase is only two days (between both Mod 2 and Mod 3) so there is a minor financial commitment of 2x days of R&Q/TD for up to 24 students ($1,756.45) - under the assumption that all students will be on TD and not prohibited posted. The TP changes require significant dev work that the staff will only be able to commit resources to in between serials; therefore, resource management and task allocation will be critical to achieve this. Implementation: The subsequent QSP document is being updated and will go to the Training Authority (TA), MPGTG for approval. The above changes made in the new QSP will be disseminated to the Course Director for execution on the next serial. Certain portions of the new TP will likely not be met in time for the next serial as there is significant development work required and it will take time, especially factoring in leave and instructor PD. Any deviations of the new TP can be mitigated and waived in accordance with Cyber Standards. |
| **QS or TP manager****: ** Capt M. Potvin – TP Manager |

### Version 2.0

| **Record of amendment – Version ****2****.0** |
| --- |
| Date | Location |
| Jan 2024 | CTU, Kingston |
| **Requirement for Amendment****:** This QSP has been amended to reflect the new changes to QSP. |
| **Substantiation**** of QS amendment****:** All changes to the QSP are driven by the realization that most of the EOs taught in PO 007 are important tasks that are considered POs on the job. The main example is that PO 007 Ver 1 “Defend the DND/CAF Cyber Domain” is a description of what the Cyber Operator trade does and doesn’t capture any discrete taskings of the cyber operators. Ver 1 EOs such as “Triage Potential Malware” is more in line with tasks that cyber operators are expected to complete on the job and are more in line with traditional POs in the CFITES system. Originally this was mitigated with the introduction of Critical ECs to add summative testing to extremely critical tasks such as “Categorize malicious activity in network traffic” and “Categorize malicious activity on hosts” creating summative tests for these EOs. Most QS changes made is in reflection of this substantiation. All QS changes do not change the training content but will add more formative and summative assessments which was identified as a requirement from After Action Reports with training staff. All training content changes can be seen on the Training Plan changes. **Description of change (s)****: **The following changes have been made to this QSP: Chapter 1/3 changes: Outline of Training: Chapter 1 para 5 updated to reflect new QS changes. Changed TE from “Canadian Forces School of Communication and Electronics (CFSCE)” to “Cyber Training Unit (CTU)” Cyber Operator training is not longer delivered by CFSCE and is now under the CTU Mod 2 and 3 have been changed to reflect new QS changes and to facilitate training into smaller chunks for Reserve Force training. New Mod 4: added to facilitate Reserve Force training and accommodate the increase in POs from QS changes Course Capacity: Chapter 3 para 9 b. has been updated for maximum load of 12 to reflect current usable numbers based on instructor and physical limitations. Chapter 3 para 10 b. has been updated to reflect instructor ratio of 4:1 from 8:1. 8:1 ratio was with the intention of TAs being supplied, due to the inability to train or staff TAs, the number has been reduced back to 4:1. Prerequisites: Added Chapter 3 para 16. Substantiation: Due to technical nature of trade and the employment of Cyber Operators in international scaled operations, most terms both in public and private sectors are in English. See remarks for further substantiation. Progress Monitoring Chapter 3 para 30 c. has been updated to reflect TA Standing Orders and results. Substantiation: Original wording followed CTC orders; new wording follows MPGTG TGSOs. Training Duration: Changed periods per day from 8 -> 7. Adjusted numbers based on new TP changes. Substantiation: Previous calculations relied on instructors working 9-hour days with lunch included; new periods per day will allow regular work hours for instructors. Course Capacity: Added Instructor/Staffing requirements including instructor to student ratio. Ideally in the future, if Training Assistants are added, this would be updated to reflect it. Ratio has been lowered back down to 1:4 due to a lack of Training Assistants. Substantiation: Instructor to student ratio is lower than standard courses due to the technical and varied nature of the course content. Each student will require assistance in various areas. Chapter 2 changes: Removed PO 006 “Apply Cyber Security Orders and Directives” Substantiation: The original content of PO 006 is a very small portion of the training in which most of the training is normally taught during indoctrination training at any operational unit operating at Level II or higher. The content remains in the course but has become a part of the TP as an EO under a new PO 006. Removed PO 007 “Defend the DND/CAF Cyber Domain” Substantiation: This PO did not describe a task of the Cyber Operators, but generically described everything that a Cyber Operator would do. The PO has been broken up into 5 POs. Added PO 006 “Analyze DND Network Traffic” Moved content of PO 006 “Apply Cyber Security Orders and Directives” to an EO in new PO 006. Moved content of EO 007.01 “Categorize normal DND network traffic” to its own PO and renamed to “Analyze DND Network Traffic” Substantiation: changed verb from “categorize normal” to “analyze” to properly reflect the task of the cyber operators. Added PO 007 “Analyze Malicious Activity in Network Traffic” moved content of EO 007.02 “Categorize malicious activity in network traffic” to new PO; changed verb from “categorize” to “analyze” to properly reflect the task; moved and shortened content of EO 007.08 “Conduct vulnerability analysis and Exploitation” to new PO Substantiation: New content fell under a “No Train” in the master task list, however the exposure of the content is important for students to continue with the PO and what they could potentially do on the job. Content of Describing adversary TTPs was moved to this PO from EO 007 “Investigate Cyber Events” Substantiation: When expecting students to analyze network traffic, an idea of adversary movement is required. Added PO 008 “Triage potential Malware moved content of EO 007.03 “Triage Potential Malware” to its own PO Added PO 009 “Analyze Malicious Activity in logs moved content of EO 007.04 “Categorize Malicious Activity in host” to its own PO and moved content of EO 007.06 “collect artifacts” into this PO moved content of EO 007.09 “categorizing malicious activity in the cloud” into this PO changed title from “categorize malicious activity in host” to “Analyze malicious activity in logs” Added PO 010 “Respond to Cyber Incidents” moved content of EO 007.07 “Investigate cyber events” to new PO; and changed verb EO 007.05 “Initiate technical report” has been included into POs 008 - 010. **Substantiation**** of TP amendment:** All changes to the TP are driven by the realignment of the QS change along with adjustments to better reflect what can be practically taught. Chapter 4 Changes: General: Assessments (Annex E): All times have been changed from 50 min periods to full length times to properly reflect testing times. All assessments have a 50 min period for dedicated review of test. Substantiation: Students have a hard time learning from formative/summative tests when there is no dedicated time for review. Instructors had to previously work outside hours or compromise on other lecture times to accommodate this. Impact Section has been updated to say “Progress Review Process will be applied to unsuccessful students IAW TE SOP’s” Substantiation: Old wording restricted TE’s ability to take each failure as a case-by-case basis. Allows to be more in line with CFITE’s guidelines. Removed all instances of CSTE workstations: Substantiation: Is not a piece of equipment utilized with the course. Once utilized an analyst workstation with CSTE network connection should be inputted. Removed all instances of SIEM software Substantiation: Intention of the Cyber Operator Pte RQ is for the students to analyze traffic and logs of a network and determine critical analysis. SIEMs are often utilized in operations but take away from training as they can conduct too much of the critical thinking of the student and impact the student’s ability to conduct the job. A properly set up SIEM is not always set up on the job. Updated Denied section to include: artificial intelligence; assistance; unapproved tools/equipment; and unapproved scripts. Training Aids section is updated to not explicitly say projector with projector screen, but as display instead. Given and Training Aids section has been updated to represent more accurately what is given to students in each EO. Substantiation: previous versions had aids and givens that were relevant in other sections, but not to the section it was a part of. An example would be pen-testing tools in the forensics section. Added Guided Discussion as teaching method to encourage student critical thinking skills. PO 006 “Analyze DND Network Traffic”: Added EO 006.01 “Comply with DND Security Orders and Directives” of which is the old PO 006 Removed Teaching Point (TPt) “Identify lead agency policies” Substantiation: Content is covered in newer TPts Added TPt “Describe DND Units, associated organizations and policies” TPt “Explain storing and handling classified material” is split into new TPts “Describe security clearance process” and “Manage classified material” Substantiation: Original TPt was too vast in content Removed TPt “Explain conducting lock-up procedures” Substantiation: Moved content to “Manage classified material. Changed EC 006.01 from written knowledge test to Game Show style. Increased time from 50 mins -> 120 mins. Substantiation: Orders and Directives are content that is often difficult for students to stay engaged and retain, changing the testing to a game show allows for a better learning experience for the students. Removed old EO 006.02 “Explain CAF Cyber doctrine and directives” Substantiation: This content consisted of reading the DAOD 6000 series and general strategic level doctrine. This was not considered necessary for the Pte RQ level of training, but these resources are available for the students to learn on their own. Removed old EO 006.03 “Explain DND networks, associated organizations and policies” Substantiation: Content has been moved to new EO 006.01 Removed EC 006.04 Substantiation: EO 006.04 has never existed in the TP. The content it tested is now reflected in new EC 006.01 Added new EO 006.02 “Identify DND Network Architecture” new content is modified content from old EO 007.01 “Categorize normal DND network traffic” Added new TPt “Review terminal commands” Substantiation: Skill is required to be reviewed due to importance and constant usage throughout the course. The contents of old TPts “Explain and demonstrate how to analyze traffic with DND analysis tools”, “Have students practice analyzing traffic with DND analysis tools”, “Explain and demonstrate how to categorize normal DND network traffic”, and “Have students practice categorizing normal DND network traffic” have been redistributed to new TPts “Describe DND Network Architecture” and “Analyze DND network devices in network traffic”. Substantiation: Effort to streamline titles, reduce redundancy of TPts between lectures/practical exercises, and adjust for flow of instruction. Added EC 006.02 “Identify DND network architecture”. Added practical, question-based test to conduct a formative test on new EO. Adjusted PC 006 from 100 mins (2x periods) to 120 mins, changed name from “Apply cyber security orders and directives” to “Analyze DND network traffic”. PC is now two-part exam that reflects both EOs in the PO. Part one is a closed book knowledge test Part two is an open book practical, question-based test Substantiation: Change in PO demands new PC that properly reflects the different sections of the PO. Time change is to properly reflect time given to students. PO 007 “Analyze Malicious Activity in Network Traffic”: old EO 007.02 “Categorize malicious activity in network traffic” is expanded on to encapsulate an entire PO and consists of three EOs “Triage IDS Alerts”, “Identify Kill Chain Activities”, and “Produce a Technical Report”. EO “Triage IDS Alerts” old TPt content “Explain and demonstrate how to triage alert data”, “have students practice triaging alert data”, “Explain and demonstrate how to investigate alert data”, “Have students practice investigating alert data”, “Explain and demonstrate how to mitigate false positives”, and “Have students practice mitigating false positives” have been redistributed to new TPts “Generate IDS Alerts”, “Categorize IDS Alerts”, “Prepare network traffic related to alert data”, and Determine validity of alerts” EO “Identify Kill Chain Activities” Added shortened content of old EO 007.08 “Conduct vulnerability analysis and exploitation” as TPt “Perform red team activities on the network” Substantiation: EO content is marked as a No Train in task disposition at Annex B. Content is still available as students will need to know how an attacker functions to identify malicious activities. Divided up old TPt “Explain and demonstrate how to categorize malicious traffic” and “Have students practice categorizing malicious traffic” into TPts “Identify reconnaissance techniques”, “Identify delivery techniques”, “Identify exploitation techniques”, and “Identify post-exploitation techniques”. Substantiation: Original two TPts were too vast, divided it up between logical points of the Cyber Kill Chain for flow of instruction. EO “Produce a Technical Report” Moved old EO 007.07 TPt content of “Describe adversary TTPs” to TPt “Describe cyber threat actors and common cyber kill chains”. Substantiation: With an earlier introduction of cyber kill chains, this content is required to be taught earlier in the course. Reintroduce “Briefing Emerging Cyber Threats” TPt Substantiation: Marked as a Train task in Annex B. Useful for students to stay up to date in the cyber field. Expectation of the job. Added “Introduction to course technical report” Added “Report on Network Analysis” Substantiation: Students require dedicated time to report on their findings. Added “Brief Technical Report” Substantiation: Ptes/Cpls are often required to brief their findings on the job. This gives familiarity during the course while also providing them an opportunity to review their own report prior to assessments. Added EC 007.01-03. Changed from old CEC #2 007.02. Time increased from 200 mins (4 periods) to 240 mins. Added 50 mins of review. Added detailed criteria of student results to reflect closer to PC. Changed from Question to Scenario based practical testing. Substantiation: Only one EC for the PO as all components of the PO is needed to properly test students’ proficiency in EOs. Question to Scenario change was made as Question based testing fails to properly test students’ ability to identify and categorize malicious activities on their own. Added PC 007. Same structure as combined EC. PO 008 “Triage Potential Malware”: old EO “Triage potential malware” is expanded on to encapsulate an entire PO with three EOs “Conduct Basic Static Analysis”, “Conduct Basic Dynamic Analysis”, and “Produce a Technical Report”. EO “Conduct Basic Static Analysis” Added TPt “Describe PEs in Windows OS” Substantiation: Critical knowledge for students to know when interacting with executables on the Windows OS. Not assumed to be taught through PO 1-5. Added TPt “Set up analysis environment” Substantiation: Skills to set up an analysis environment is covered in PO 004, but these covers setting one up for malware analysis. Moved Parts of old TPt “Explain and demonstrate how to triage potential malware” and “Have students practice triaging potential malware” into new TPts “Describe automated analysis tools”, “Hash binaries”, and “Analyze static information of binaries” Substantiation: Original TPt covered an entire EO, split up between the new EOs with broken down TPts. Added EC 008.01 “Conduct basic static analysis on binaries” EO “Conduct Basic Dynamic Analysis” Added TPt “Set up dynamic analysis environment” Moved Parts of old TPt “Explain and demonstrate how to triage potential malware” and “Have students practice triaging potential malware” into new TPts “Analyze dynamic behaviour of binaries” Added TPt “Other malicious files” Substantiation: Common for Ptes/Cpls to encounter other files such as pdfs, docs, etc. and will be required to triage those as well. These files are the similar steps with different tools. Added EC 008.02 “Conduct basic dynamic analysis” Added EC 008.01-03 “Triage potential malware” Substantiation: An overall EC is required to formatively test students on their ability to piece together the entire task. Added PC 008 “Triage potential malware” PO 009 “Analyze Malicious Activity in Logs”: PO is a combination of old EO 007.04 “Categorize malicious activity on hosts”, EO 007.06 “Collect Artifacts”, and EO 007.09 “Categorize malicious activity in the cloud”. These have been reorganized and renamed to EO 009.01 “Collect Forensic Artifacts”, EO 009.02 “Analyze Activities in Logs”, EO 009.03 “Analyze Activities in Cloud”, and EO 009.04 “Produce a Technical Report” Substantiation: EO contents are similar in skills or related in parts of an investigations. Name change is to properly reflect content and related task. EO “Collect Forensic Artifacts” Renaming of TPts, but virtually no change in content. EC #5 007.06 “Safeguard forensic evidence” changed to EC 009.01 “Collect forensic artifacts”. Time increased from 300 mins (6 x 50 mins) to 180 mins with 50 mins of review. Format of EC has not changed. Substantiation: Students consistently finish the test material in less then half the periods. Test was adjusted to reflect this. EO “Analyze Activities in Logs” Drastic name change is provided as students could be provided any types of logs from different devices such as routers, email servers, etc. Although “hosts” can cover this definition the name was changed to “Logs” to ensure that it captures all possible logs that come from different devices and services. The two old TPts “Explain and demonstrate categorizing malicious host log activity” and “Have students practice categorize malicious host log activity” have been divided up into “Windows Event Logs”, “Sysmon”, “Identify Different Activities”, and “Identify Malicious Activities”. Small parts of the content were removed or moved as other POs naturally cover the topic. Substantiation: Original TPts covered the entire EO, broke apart for better flow in instruction. Added EC “Analyze activities in logs” EO “Analyze Activities in Cloud” Removed many parts of TPt “Internet facing security concerns” Substantiation: Most content is covered in previous POs. This EO does not add anything new to those topics. Modified EC #7 007.09 “Analyze malicious cloud activity” to EC 009.03 “Analyze activities in cloud”. Changed test from practical to written knowledge test. Changed time of test from 400 mins (8 periods) to 60 mins and 50 mins of review. Substantiation: Lack of resources to have a practical assessment procured and update with future tests if necessary. Risk is mitigated as it is similar to Log and Network Analysis. Added PC 009 “Analyze Malicious Activity in Logs” PO 010 “Respond to Cyber Incidents”: old EO “Investigate Cyber Events” is expanded on to encapsulate an entire PO and is divided into two EOs: “Reconstruct Malicious Incident Timeline” and “Produce a Complete Technical Report”. Substantiation: This PO encapsulates the entire training and acts as a culmination of the previous POs by combining the different parts. EO “Reconstruct Malicious Incident Timeline” Added TPt “Describe relations between disciplines” Substantiation: Students are taught how to combine the different parts of analysis and how they can aid each other in a complete investigation TPts “Explain and demonstrate how to reconstruct malicious activity” and “Have students practice reconstructing malicious activity” have been combined to a new singular TPt “Reconstruct malicious activity”. Substantiation: Effort to reduce redundancy in TP and streamline titles. No EC is required for this PO as the entire PO is full of practice runs that all act as formative checkpoints for students. Old PC 007 “Defend the DND/CAF Cyber Domain” has been modified to be PC 010 “Respond to Cyber Incidents”. As it is a final exercise, time has been added to include Testing, Correction, Review/Remedial, and Retest. Removed Exercise Title. Modified Criterion of the test to not be as restrictive but stays true to the original intent. Substantiation: Original Criterion made it too difficult to produce new tests that are varied and different. New criterion allows for variety in actions seen but will still maintain the same level of time and standard for each test to stay consistent. PO 099 “Comply with the CAF Code of Values and Ethics”: Removed PO as Chapter 3 “Removal from Training” outlines student expectations. Students are all subject to the DAODs, QR&Os, Code of Service Discipline, etc. at all times. Annex C Changes: Updated table to reflect change of length. Updated table to reflect assumptions on student locations Students now attend CAF portion after being posted to BTL Kingston. Previously students were a part of BTL Ottawa and would take TD for the course. Updated table to reflect change in college program Increased Max load from 24 students to 48 students. Through the contract, accommodations are provided to the students while they are attending the college program. Previously this did not exist. Updated table to reflect course load max to be 12 students for CAF portion. Substantiation: Due to technical nature of the course, quality of instruction and learning environment increases by running multiple serials of 12 students at a time. Annex D Changes: Updated timetable to reflect each Module 2-4 separately. Intention is to have Module 2-4 run one after the other as one serial. Annex E Changes: Changes can be seen in Chapter 4 changes section. Annex F Changes: Table updated to reflect new POs in QSP Implementation: The subsequent QSP document is being updated and will go to the Training Authority (TA), MPGTG for approval. The above changes made in the new QSP will be disseminated to the Course Director for execution on the next serial. Certain portions of the new TP will likely not be met in time for the next serial as there is significant development work required and it will take time, especially factoring in leave and instructor PD. Any deviations of the new TP can be mitigated and waived in accordance with Cyber Standards. **QS or TP Manager:** TP Manager: Capt K. Kim TDO: Capt C. Gosselin |

### Version 2.1

| **Record of amendment – Version 2.****1** |
| --- |
| Date | Location |
| 30 November 2024 | CTU, Kingston |
| **Requirement for Amendment****:** This QS/TP has been amended to reflect the new changes to QS/TP. |
| **Substantiation**** of QS****/TP**** amendment:** With the lack of proper standards control on the contracted college of Module 1 an assessment to ensure that students are ready for later Modules of the course was required. With the increasing backlog and long wait times of the course, this assessment is designed to combat skill-fade in technical knowledge/skills. **Description of change (s)****: **The following changes have been made to this QSP: Included the Foundational Knowledge and Skills Assessment (FKSA) to the Training Strategy in between Module 1 and Module 2 of the Pte RQ. Added para 17. In Prerequisites that a student must have successful FKSA to be loaded onto Module 2-4 of the Pte RQ. The qualification should expire after a year due to skill-fade. Assessment Plan: Added the FKSA description: Summative 2 part assessment, 4 hours each Both closed book 1 knowledge test with multiple choice, short answer, fill in the blank that covers key knowledge components of Module 1. 1 practical test with short answer questions that require students to practically demonstrate skills and understanding of Module 1. This is not an equivalent to Module 1 Due to resources and time, this assessment does not cover the entirety of Module 1 and cannot be utilized as a PLAR. The uncovered parts are still a requirement of the Cyber Operators Adjusted the delivery of PC 006 (open -> closed book) Justification: Students are expected to conduct the test without internet. It was determined that with internet and notes, students could be passing the test without being able to conduct the task on their own. Adjusted the delivery of PC 010: Removed SITREPS and instead of a report every day, a single report at the end of the last day is required. Justification (SITREPS): It has been noted that SITREPS were redundant with the current testing material and was taking time away from students conducting the test. The original goal of the SITREPS was not to assess briefing, but to aid in circumventing less then ideal testing material. The testing material has since been resolved. Justification (Reports): To reflect more what the students will do operationally at the job. The two reports were considered a training limitation as students could talk in between testing days. However, as long as the testing material is introduced on separate days and no notes are allowed to exit/enter the testing area, the potential academic integrity is mitigated as the students who do poorly on the first day will need to spend additional time finalizing earlier days before moving onto the next day. Invigilation of the test will need to be increased for subsequent testing days. **QS or ****or TP Manager:** TP Manager: Capt K. Kim TDO: Capt C. Gosselin |

